Armada on the AYN Odin / Odin Pro (Snapdragon 845)
==================================================

Unlike Armada's SM8550+ devices, the original Odin boots Linux through
U-Boot: the stock Android bootloader (ABL) chain-loads a U-Boot image from
one of the Android A/B boot slots, and U-Boot provides UEFI to boot Armada's
GRUB from this SD card. Android stays on the other slot — switch slots to
switch OS.

U-Boot images in this folder (from armada-packages/uboot-odin, based on
https://github.com/sigmaris/u-boot 'odin' branch):

  uboot-ayn-odin.img     Odin / Odin Pro (and Odin Base)
  uboot-ayn-odin-m2.img  Odin M2 only

The Odin Lite is MediaTek-based and is NOT supported.

One-time flash (needs a PC with fastboot, USB-C cable):

1. Copy this folder's .img files to the PC.

2. Power off the Odin. Hold VOL- and plug in USB-C (or hold VOL- + POWER)
   until the green START/fastboot screen appears.

3. Find the active Android slot:

     fastboot getvar current-slot

   The commands below assume the answer was "a", so Linux goes to slot "b".
   If it said "b", swap a and b throughout.

4. Erase the dtbo of the Linux slot (Android would otherwise apply its
   device tree overlays over U-Boot's device tree and break it):

     fastboot erase dtbo_b

5. Tethered test boot first (nothing is flashed yet; SD card inserted):

     fastboot boot uboot-ayn-odin.img

   If U-Boot appears on screen and boots Armada from SD, make it permanent:

     fastboot flash boot_b uboot-ayn-odin.img
     fastboot --set-active=b
     fastboot reboot

Switching between Armada and Android:

  Boot to fastboot and run `fastboot --set-active=a` (Android) or
  `fastboot --set-active=b` (Armada). The Project Valhalla multiboot ABL
  (github.com/ProjectValhalla) can also be used for an on-device menu.

Going back to stock:

  Nothing on internal storage is modified except the Linux slot's boot and
  dtbo partitions, which held only an inactive copy of Android's kernel.
  `fastboot --set-active=a` restores Android; reflashing the stock boot/dtbo
  images to slot b (from AYN's firmware packages) fully reverts it.

Notes:

  - Screen support currently targets the V1 panel (Innolux TD4328). V2-screen
    units may show no display under Linux yet.
  - The /KERNEL file used by SM8550+ devices is not used on the Odin; the
    EFI folder on this SD card is the boot path.
